{
  "email": "dana.whitfield@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Identify sender, department/title for software approval per KB-027"
}

{
  "query": "PDF editor Company Portal approved",
  "reason": "Check whether an approved PDF editor is available in Company Portal"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Dana, thanks for checking with us before installing this. We can't install software from a download link sent by a vendor, because files like that haven't been checked and are a common way malware gets in. Please don't run that file yourself either. Here's the quickest way to get a PDF editor: 1) Open the Company Portal (Software Center) on your laptop and search for \"PDF\". Any app listed there is already approved, and you can install it yourself in a few clicks. 2) If nothing there does what you need, reply with the PDF tasks you need to do (for example editing text, signing, or merging files). We'll then send a request for a security review of an approved PDF editor and handle the install for you. One more thing: if you've already downloaded or opened that setup file, please tell us right away so we can check your laptop.",
  "internal_note": "User: Dana Whitfield, CEO, Executive, VIP. Device BL-LT-0101. She asked us to install a 'free PDF editor' from an executable link a vendor sent her (http://free-pdf-tools.example/setup.exe). Per KB-027 we do not install software from links received by email, and we do not grant local admin. I declined the install. I sent her to the Company Portal for an approved PDF app. If none fits, the request needs a security review. As CEO she has no manager to approve, so the approver path may need to go to Security or Tier 2 once she gives a business reason. I asked her to say if she downloaded or ran the file. If she did, treat it as possible malware (KB-029 and a device check). I can't see the Company Portal catalog from here, so I couldn't confirm whether a PDF editor is already published. It's worth considering a report to Security about the vendor link, since unsolicited executables sent to a VIP are a known risk. No actions queued.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-027"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
